---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SeoProductBridge from '../components/SeoProductBridge.astro';
import '../styles/v23.css';

const title = 'Dream Journal App for Voice Capture, Recall & Dream History | Somnori';
const description = 'Somnori includes a dream journal layer for voice capture, original audio, Morning review and recurring dream history inside a broader nighttime system.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} canonical="/dream-journal" type="software">
  <section class="article-hero">
    <div class="article-shell wide">
      <div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/">Somnori</a> / Dream Journal</div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Dream journal</p>
      <h1>A Dream Journal Designed Around the Night Itself</h1>
      <p class="lead">Somnori's dream journal starts before the morning entry: capture while the memory is alive, return in Morning, and let recurring context accumulate over time.</p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="button primary" href="/voice-dream-journal">Explore voice capture</a>
        <a class="button secondary" href="/remember-dreams">Improve dream recall</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-tight">
    <div class="article-shell wide">
      <div class="loop">
        <div class="loop-item"><b>Before sleep</b><span>Prepare sound, practice or recall intention.</span></div>
        <div class="loop-item"><b>Night</b><span>Capture a dream with less deliberate interaction.</span></div>
        <div class="loop-item"><b>Morning</b><span>Review the night's entries while fully awake.</span></div>
        <div class="loop-item"><b>History</b><span>Keep recurring dream context connected over time.</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <SeoProductBridge
    focus="Dream journal"
    variant="dreams"
    title="The dream journal is a major Somnori layer — not the boundary of the product."
    text="A person can use Somnori for dreams, for night thoughts and ideas, for asking the time or setting an alarm, or for a chosen bedtime routine. The dream journal gives one of those workflows a dedicated memory and pattern layer."
  />

  <article class="article-shell prose section-tight">
    <h2>What should a modern dream journal actually solve?</h2>
    <p>Most dream journals are good at storing an entry once you have already managed to capture it. The harder problem comes earlier: remembering enough of the dream while half awake and getting it into the journal without replacing it with the interface itself.</p>
    <p>Somnori therefore treats capture, Morning and longer-term dream context as one dream workflow rather than separate features.</p>

    <h2>Night capture first</h2>
    <p>The dream workflow is voice-first. A dream does not need to be written as an essay. It can begin as a rough audio entry: a room, a person, an impossible event, a feeling. The original recording remains useful because it preserves how you described the dream before editing or reinterpretation.</p>
    <p>If you want the specific flow, see the <a href="/hands-free-dream-journal">hands-free dream journal</a> page. If you are comparing voice with typing, see the <a href="/voice-dream-journal">voice dream journal</a>.</p>

    <h2>Morning is a different job</h2>
    <p>At night, the interface should demand as little attention as possible. In the morning, you can afford context: what was recorded, when it happened, which entries were dreams, and what you still want to add.</p>
    <p>The current Somnori Morning experience is the first implementation of that separation. It brings together the completed night and the entries that can be reviewed after waking.</p>

    <h2>From entries to recurring context</h2>
    <p>A single dream can be interesting. A history of dreams can be more useful because recurring people, places, emotions and impossible situations begin to have personal context.</p>
    <p>Somnori already has a dream-sign layer in the current product direction. The larger Dream Intelligence layer remains a roadmap item and should stay clearly separated from shipped behavior.</p>

    <h2>Dream signs and lucid dreaming</h2>
    <p>For lucid dreamers, recurring cues can become practical dream signs. A personalized system can eventually connect techniques to what actually appears in your own journal rather than to a generic list of symbols.</p>
    <p>This is the direction behind Somnori's lucid layer: personal history first, technique second.</p>

    <h2>What about AI dream interpretation?</h2>
    <p>AI can help summarize, ask reflection questions and surface possible themes. It should not pretend that a universal symbol dictionary can diagnose the meaning of a dream. Somnori's planned AI layer is intended to be cautious, contextual and grounded in the user's own history.</p>
    <p>A one-off AI interpreter can be useful for acquisition and reflection. It is not the whole product.</p>

    <h2>The important change: dreams are not the only reason to use Somnori</h2>
    <p>The same night that contains a dream can also contain an idea you do not want to lose, a question about the time, an alarm change, a chosen piece of music or a self-suggestion routine before sleep. Those are not distractions from the product anymore; they are part of the product architecture.</p>

    <div class="product-scope-grid">
      <div><b>Capture layer</b><span>Dreams, thoughts and ideas that arrive at night.</span></div>
      <div><b>Night-control layer</b><span>Hands-free time and alarm commands inside the same context.</span></div>
      <div><b>Routine layer</b><span>Music, practices and autosuggestion before sleep, with a future wake protocol.</span></div>
    </div>

    <h2>Somnori vs. a generic notes app</h2>
    <table class="compare-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Need</th><th>Generic notes app</th><th>Somnori</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Write a daytime note</td><td>Excellent</td><td>Not the primary job</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Capture a night thought or idea</td><td>Usually manual navigation</td><td>Part of the nighttime capture layer</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Capture a dream while half asleep</td><td>Usually manual navigation</td><td>Dream-specific night workflow</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Keep original dream audio</td><td>Depends on app</td><td>Part of the dream-entry workflow</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Morning review of one night</td><td>Folder/list based</td><td>Dedicated Morning experience</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Dream signs / lucid layer</td><td>Not dream-specific</td><td>Connected to dream history</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Privacy matters here</h2>
    <p>Dreams and night thoughts can contain unusually intimate material: relationships, fears, private memories, health concerns, unfinished ideas or personal plans. Data handling therefore belongs in the product design, not only in a legal footer.</p>
    <p>The public site will keep specific security, export and storage claims conservative until they are verified in the shipped build.</p>

    <h2>Who the dream-journal layer is for</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>People who want a dream journal but dislike typing immediately after waking.</li>
      <li>People who wake during the night and want to preserve dreams without fully engaging with the phone.</li>
      <li>People interested in recurring dream patterns rather than isolated dictionary meanings.</li>
      <li>Lucid dreamers who want practice connected to their own journal history.</li>
      <li>People who want dream capture to coexist with the rest of their nighttime routine.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>The journal is the dream-memory layer</h2>
    <p>Somnori is broader than a journal. The dream journal is the specialized memory layer for people who care about dreams: it connects night capture, Morning, history, recurring signs and the future lucid/intelligence layer. Someone who does not care about dreams can still use other parts of Somnori without entering this workflow at all.</p>
  </article>

  <section class="section-tight">
    <div class="article-shell wide callout">
      <p class="eyebrow">Start with the night</p>
      <h2>The first useful dream is the one you actually save.</h2>
      <p class="lead">See how Somnori reduces the interaction between waking from a dream and preserving it.</p>
      <div class="actions"><a class="button primary" href="/hands-free-dream-journal">Hands-free dream capture</a></div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
